<h2>1. What is a Samāsa?</h2>
<p>
  A <strong>Samāsa (समास)</strong> is a compound word: two or more independent words joined into a single word. The traditional definition is <em>samasanaṃ samāsaḥ</em>, "putting together is samāsa". When words are compounded, the case endings of all members except the last are normally dropped, and the whole compound then declines like a single word.
</p>
<p>
  Sanskrit uses compounds far more than most languages. A single compound in classical literature can replace an entire clause, which is why recognising and analysing compounds is essential for reading texts and a regular question in school examinations.
</p>
<p>
  The process of breaking a compound back into its words, with their proper case endings, is called <strong>Vigraha (विग्रह)</strong>. For example, the compound <code>राजपुरुषः</code> has the vigraha <code>राज्ञः पुरुषः</code>, "the king's man".
</p>

<h2>2. The Six Main Types of Samāsa</h2>
<p>
  Compounds are classified by which member is the most important (<em>pradhāna</em>) in meaning. A useful summary:
</p>
<Table
  headers={["Samāsa", "Most important member", "Example", "Vigraha"]}
  rows={[
    ["Avyayībhāva", "First (an indeclinable)", "यथाशक्ति", "शक्तिम् अनतिक्रम्य"],
    ["Tatpuruṣa", "Second", "राजपुरुषः", "राज्ञः पुरुषः"],
    ["Karmadhāraya", "Second (adjective + noun)", "नीलकमलम्", "नीलं कमलम्"],
    ["Dvigu", "Second (number first)", "त्रिलोकी", "त्रयाणां लोकानां समाहारः"],
    ["Dvandva", "All members", "रामलक्ष्मणौ", "रामः च लक्ष्मणः च"],
    ["Bahuvrīhi", "Something outside the compound", "पीताम्बरः", "पीतम् अम्बरं यस्य सः"],
  ]}
/>
<p>
  Strictly speaking, Karmadhāraya and Dvigu are sub-types of Tatpuruṣa, but schools usually teach them separately because they are so common.
</p>

<h2>3. Avyayībhāva: Compounds That Become Indeclinable</h2>
<p>
  In an Avyayībhāva compound the first member is an indeclinable (<em>avyaya</em>) such as <code>यथा</code>, <code>उप</code> or <code>प्रति</code>, and the whole compound behaves as an indeclinable, usually ending in <code>-म्</code>.
</p>
<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
  <li><code>उपगङ्गम्</code> = <code>गङ्गायाः समीपम्</code> (near the Ganga)</li>
  <li><code>प्रतिदिनम्</code> = <code>दिनं दिनं प्रति</code> (every day)</li>
  <li><code>यथाशक्ति</code> = <code>शक्तिम् अनतिक्रम्य</code> (according to one's ability)</li>
</ul>

<h2>4. Tatpuruṣa, Karmadhāraya and Dvigu</h2>
<p>
  In a <strong>Tatpuruṣa</strong> the second word carries the main meaning, and the first word stands in a case relationship to it. The case used in the vigraha gives the sub-type its name:
</p>
<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
  <li><code>ग्रामगतः</code> = <code>ग्रामं गतः</code> (gone to the village, 2nd case)</li>
  <li><code>चौरभयम्</code> = <code>चौरात् भयम्</code> (fear of thieves, 5th case)</li>
  <li><code>विद्यालयः</code> = <code>विद्यायाः आलयः</code> (house of learning, 6th case)</li>
</ul>
<p>
  A <strong>Karmadhāraya</strong> joins words that refer to the same thing, typically an adjective and a noun, or a comparison: <code>नीलकमलम्</code> = <code>नीलं कमलम्</code> (blue lotus); <code>घनश्यामः</code> = <code>घनः इव श्यामः</code> (dark like a cloud).
</p>
<p>
  A <strong>Dvigu</strong> is a Karmadhāraya whose first member is a number, usually denoting a group: <code>त्रिलोकी</code> (the three worlds), <code>पञ्चवटी</code> (a group of five banyan trees), <code>चतुर्युगम्</code> (the four ages).
</p>

<h2>5. Dvandva: Compounds of Equals</h2>
<p>
  In a Dvandva every member is equally important, joined by an implied <code>च</code> (and).
</p>
<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
  <li><strong>Itaretara Dvandva</strong> keeps each member distinct, so the number reflects the total: <code>रामलक्ष्मणौ</code> = <code>रामः च लक्ष्मणः च</code> (dual).</li>
  <li><strong>Samāhāra Dvandva</strong> treats the members as one collective, neuter singular: <code>पाणिपादम्</code> = <code>पाणी च पादौ च, तेषां समाहारः</code> (hands and feet).</li>
</ul>

<h2>6. Bahuvrīhi: Compounds That Describe Someone Else</h2>
<p>
  A Bahuvrīhi compound refers to something that is <em>not</em> named in it. Its vigraha uses a relative pronoun such as <code>यस्य</code> (whose) or <code>यः</code> (who).
</p>
<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
  <li><code>पीताम्बरः</code> = <code>पीतम् अम्बरं यस्य सः</code> (he whose garment is yellow, i.e. Kṛṣṇa)</li>
  <li><code>दशाननः</code> = <code>दश आननानि यस्य सः</code> (he who has ten faces, i.e. Rāvaṇa)</li>
  <li><code>लम्बोदरः</code> = <code>लम्बम् उदरं यस्य सः</code> (he whose belly is large, i.e. Gaṇeśa)</li>
</ul>
<p>
  The same compound can be read two ways. <code>नीलकण्ठः</code> as a Karmadhāraya means "a blue throat" (<code>नीलः कण्ठः</code>); as a Bahuvrīhi it means "he whose throat is blue", i.e. Śiva (<code>नीलः कण्ठः यस्य सः</code>). Context decides.
</p>

<h2>7. A Method for Any Compound</h2>
<ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
  <li>Undo any sandhi at the junction (e.g. <code>लम्बोदर</code> → <code>लम्ब + उदर</code>).</li>
  <li>Ask whether the first member is an indeclinable (Avyayībhāva) or a number (Dvigu).</li>
  <li>Ask whether the members are equals joined by "and" (Dvandva).</li>
  <li>Ask whether the compound describes something outside itself (Bahuvrīhi).</li>
  <li>Otherwise it is a Tatpuruṣa or Karmadhāraya: find the case that links the words.</li>
</ol>
<p>
  Practise these steps on the compounds in your textbook chapters, then test yourself with the grammar exercises in Sanskritbhashi's School Prep module.
</p>
